<p>
  India has a long history of making electrical test and measuring instruments, and buyers in the Gulf, Africa and elsewhere increasingly source from Indian manufacturers and distributors. Importing instruments is straightforward once you know what to ask. This guide lists the questions that protect you, whichever supplier you use.
</p>

<h2 id="specs-first">Start with the Specification, Not the Price</h2>

<p>
  Write down the measurements you need, the ranges, the accuracy, the test voltages and the standards your clients require. Two instruments with similar names can differ greatly in range and rating. Ask each supplier for the full datasheet and compare like for like. Our <a href={u('/blog/accuracy-vs-resolution-vs-precision/')}>accuracy, resolution and precision guide</a> helps read specs fairly.
</p>

<h2 id="questions">Ten Questions to Ask Any Supplier</h2>

<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 my-4">
  <li>Which standards does the instrument conform to, and can I see the document or test report?</li>
  <li>What is its measurement category and voltage rating, if it connects to live circuits?</li>
  <li>What supply voltage and frequency does it need, if mains-powered?</li>
  <li>Does it ship with a calibration or test certificate, and what does that certificate cover?</li>
  <li>Who manufactured it, and who is the importer or marketer of record?</li>
  <li>What is the warranty, and who handles a claim from my country?</li>
  <li>Are leads, probes, spare fuses and consumables available later?</li>
  <li>Is the manual in the language my users need?</li>
  <li>How is it packed for sea or air freight, and what is the weight and volume?</li>
  <li>What is the realistic lead time, from confirmed order to dispatch?</li>
</ol>

<Callout type="info" title="Manufacturer or trader">
  Both are legitimate, but know which you are dealing with. A manufacturer can explain design and spare parts directly, while a distributor can offer a wider range from several makers. Reputable suppliers state clearly which products they make and which they distribute.
</Callout>

<h2 id="calibration-traceability">Calibration and Traceability</h2>

<p>
  Many buyers, especially in regulated industries and for tender work, need a calibration certificate traceable to national or international standards. Ask exactly what is offered and by whom, because a factory test certificate and an accredited laboratory certificate are not the same thing. Our guide on <a href={u('/blog/nabl-vs-manufacturer-calibration/')}>accredited versus manufacturer calibration</a> explains the difference, and the <a href={u('/blog/calibration-interval-guide/')}>calibration interval guide</a> covers how often to repeat it.
</p>

<h2 id="shipping-terms">Shipping Terms and Paperwork</h2>

<p>
  Agree the delivery terms clearly: who arranges freight and insurance, and where the risk passes. Common trade terms include EXW, FOB, CFR and CIF, and the right choice depends on whether you or the supplier has the better freight arrangements. Electrical measuring instruments generally fall under the customs heading 9030, but the exact subheading and duty depend on the instrument and your country, so confirm with your customs broker before shipping. The supplier should provide a commercial invoice, packing list and certificate of origin on request.
</p>

<h2 id="after-sales">Plan for After-Sales</h2>

<p>
  An instrument is a long-term tool. Ask how you will get service, spares and recalibration five years from now. Hand-driven and mechanical designs are valued in remote locations because there is little to fail and they can be repaired locally. See <a href={u('/blog/digital-vs-analog-earth-tester/')}>digital versus analog earth testers</a> for the trade-offs.
</p>

